  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Legible Ledger",
    "overview": "Pulse replaces a spreadsheet, and the people using it read it as a spreadsheet. S-10, S-14 and S-20 are mostly figures. So it should feel like a well-kept ledger rather than a product page: dense rows, figures aligned down the column, and nothing decorative competing with the numbers for attention.\n\nThe interface stays near-neutral and quiet. One citrus accent hue carries action, and colour is otherwise held in reserve for status — where it has to mean something specific, and where spending it elsewhere would make it mean less. Headings are deliberately undersized relative to the data, because the numbers are the content and the chrome is not.\n\nDensity is a feature, not a compromise. An OFFICE_ADMIN correcting a day's attendance should reach it in three clicks (NFR-1), and every screen states what its numbers mean rather than assuming (NFR-2).",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Near-neutral greys; a single restrained citrus accent, in either scheme",
      "Two colour schemes: hue is constant, lightness flips",
      "Flat surfaces separated by 1px borders, never by shadow",
      "Dense by default — small tables, small fields, small chips",
      "Tabular figures so a column of durations or balances aligns on the decimal",
      "Status always carries an icon and a word, never colour alone",
      "A visible focus ring on everything focusable"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Reserved Colour Rule",
        "body": "Colour is spent on status and on the one action accent. A screen that uses the accent decoratively has spent it, and the next genuinely urgent thing on that screen has nothing left to say it with.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never-Colour-Alone Rule",
        "body": "Every status chip renders an icon and a written label. Each statusX variant publishes a --status-icon-gap custom property that reserves the icon’s space, so a chip built without an icon is visibly wrong rather than quietly inaccessible.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One-File Rule",
        "body": "Every hex in the application lives in app/theme/colors.js. A component that needs a colour selects a theme variant; it does not name a colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Both-Schemes Rule",
        "body": "A token that carries text is defined in both lightColors and darkColors, and the two objects are required to hold identical keys. A component needing a scheme-dependent style uses theme.applyStyles(‘dark’, …) or theme.vars, and never spells out the selector — that would be a second place for the scheme to be configured.",
        "section": "colors"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put every hex in app/theme/colors.js and select it through a theme variant.",
      "Do add a new token to both lightColors and darkColors, or to neither.",
      "Do reach for theme.vars or theme.applyStyles for anything scheme-dependent.",
      "Do keep the accent for action, and status colour for status."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't build a status style map in sx — select a statusX chip variant.",
      "Don't set fontSize, fontWeight or fontFamily outside the theme.",
      "Don't spell out the dark-scheme selector; ask the theme for it.",
      "Don't convey any state by colour alone.",
      "Don't add a second radius. There is one, and it is 6px."
    ]
